t(45, 1770, """A dialogue between me and an "AI"! Its reply was:
--------------------------------
Your analysis is entirely correct; the first lighting in the history of the West relied entirely on coal, but in two technologically different ways:
The first was lighting by gas extracted from coal (before the invention of electricity).
The second was primitive electrical stations that ran on coal.

Here is the full story of how this process went historically, how those stations were built, and how far it can be repeated in the future:
--------------------------------
## First: The first stage (lighting the streets with gas, not electricity)
In the early nineteenth century (around 1800 and after), electricity had not yet been invented for general use. The streets of London and Paris were lit by what is known as "coal gas" (Coal Gas).

* How was it done?
Hard coal was placed inside closed, huge furnaces and compressed at very high temperature without oxygen (a process called destructive distillation). As a result of this heat, the coal decomposed and released a flammable gas.
* Distribution: This gas was purified and then pumped through iron pipes under the ground to reach the lighting posts in the streets and houses, where a worker went round the streets (the lamplighter) to light them by hand every evening.
--------------------------------
## Second: The second stage (primitive coal electrical stations)
In the late nineteenth century (in 1882), the famous scientist Thomas Edison opened the first commercial electricity generating station in the world in New York (the Pearl Street Station), followed by similar stations in London. These stations relied entirely on hard coal to produce electricity, and were built by engineering methods""")

t(46, 60, """simple and primitive compared with today:

## How were these primitive stations built?
The station consisted of three main parts built of steel and firebrick:

1. The furnace and the boiler (The Boiler): A very huge brick furnace into which coal is thrown by hand with iron shovels by workers (the stokers). Above this furnace there are huge water pipes. When the coal burns, the water boils and turns into steam of high pressure and temperature.
2. The steam engine (Steam Engine): This pressurised steam is directed through strong pipes to push the pistons of a huge steam engine (resembling the engine of old trains) or a primitive turbine, which turns an iron shaft at great speed.
3. The dynamo or generator (The Dynamo): The rotating shaft connects to a primitive electrical generator (a giant magnet rotating around copper coils), and as a result of this circular motion electricity is generated.

These stations produced direct current (DC) covering an area of only one kilometre around the station, because electricity weakened as the distance increased, and the wires were strung randomly over houses and streets to light Edison's incandescent bulbs.
--------------------------------
## Third: Can this primitive matter be realised in the future if the world loses oil?
Yes, it can be realised fully and with engineering ease.
If the world loses oil, gas and complex technology as a result of a war or disaster, returning to primitive coal technology to light houses and streets is the fastest and most realistic option for humanity, for the following reasons:

* Simplicity of the engineering concept: The technology of steam engines and primitive generators has become "deep-rooted human knowledge" written in books, and does not need electronic chips or computers to run it. Any skilled blacksmith and engineer can build a steam turbine and a copper dynamo using basic metals (iron and copper).
* Abundance of raw material: Hard coal is found in layers close to the earth's surface in many countries (such as America, China, India and Australia) and can be extracted with primitive digging tools without the need for complex digital oil rigs.
* Flexibility of operation: Primitive steam stations can be run not only with hard coal, but with anything that burns and provides heat to boil water, such as wood, charcoal or agricultural waste.

Summary:
History repeats itself; the coal that brought humanity out of the dark ages in the first industrial revolution is itself the primitive "technological lifeboat" on which humanity can rely to re-light cities if the modern world collapses and we lose oil.""")

h(46, 1700, """The collapse of electrical technology is evidence of the annihilation of humans, with very few of them remaining, who find no use in reproducing it!""")

t(46, 2210, """Merely copied historical information, but in it is a great lesson that the one who perishes does not heed! Survival from death is not always evidence of the love of Allah the Exalted!
--------------------------
The copied historical information

History is full of stories of tyrants who passed through stages of perishing and near-certain death; among them were those who faced death sentences, and among them those who almost died by drowning or from illness in dark dungeons, but they survived in strange ways to become later among the worst oppressors in history.

Here are the most prominent documented historical examples:""")

t(47, 80, """🏛️ 1. The Roman emperor "Julius Caesar" (he was kidnapped and was about to be killed)

The stage before rule: In his youth, Julius Caesar fell captive to the pirates of the Mediterranean. The pirates demanded a huge ransom to release him, and death was the expected fate for him if the ransom was not paid or if they decided to get rid of him.

Empowerment and oppression: Caesar survived after the ransom was paid, and as soon as they released him he prepared an army, seized those pirates and crucified them all. Later he led wars, swept to power in Rome, appointed himself absolute dictator, and wrought havoc on earth with wars and expansions in which much blood flowed.

💀 2. The Soviet leader "Joseph Stalin" (he survived exile and death by freezing)

The stage before rule: Before the Bolshevik Revolution, Stalin was a poor young man pursued by the Tsar's secret police. He was arrested and exiled to frozen Siberia several times. In those camps, death from severe cold, hunger, or the guards' bullets was expected and almost certain for every prisoner.

Empowerment and oppression: Stalin survived and escaped from exile several times until he reached the rule of the Soviet Union. After his empowerment he became one of the bloodiest rulers in modern history, caused the killing and famine of millions of his people, and exiled hundreds of thousands to the very frozen camps he had survived.

📜 3. The Abbasid ruler "Abu Ja'far al-Mansur" (he survived the sword of the Umayyads)

The stage before rule: In the last days of the Umayyad state, the caliphate pursued all the men of Banu al-Abbas to kill them and exterminate their call. Imam Ibrahim (al-Mansur's brother) was arrested and killed in prison, and Abu Ja'far al-Mansur was wandering aimlessly, hiding and concealed from place to place, facing the danger of death and arrest at every moment.

Empowerment and oppression: The Umayyad state fell, al-Mansur survived and assumed the caliphate, founded Baghdad and strengthened the state. But as soon as Allah enabled him, he struck terribly at everyone who stood in his way; he killed his friends and old allies who had helped him reach rule (such as the commander Abu Muslim al-Khurasani), and imprisoned and killed many of the family of the Prophet's house and his opponents in cold blood.

🪓 4. The Italian dictator "Benito Mussolini" (a sentence of execution and homelessness)

The stage before rule: In his youth, Mussolini fled to Switzerland to avoid military service, and lived there as a poor homeless man sleeping under bridges; the Swiss police arrested and expelled him several times. In Italy he was sentenced to prison in absentia, and he was almost executed or died of hunger and homelessness on more than one occasion.

Empowerment and oppression: He returned and was pardoned, then founded the Fascist Party and led a march with which he crushed democracy to become the absolute ruler of Italy. He allied with Hitler and dragged his country and the world into a destructive war, oppressed his people, and used poison gases against civilians in Libya and Ethiopia.

💡 The historical wisdom

These stories confirm a cosmic law: that trial and hardship are not exclusive to the righteous; rather, tyrants too may pass through them as a kind of Divine "gradual leading-on", as they survive death so that the proof is established against them, until, when Allah seizes them after their oppression and tyranny, He seizes them with the seizing of the Mighty, the All-Capable.""")

h(47, 1900, """The dangers of perishing are not exclusive to the righteous! Tyrants: their perishing is deferred!""")

